1
00:00:04,460 --> 00:00:05,240
The next

2
00:00:15,374 --> 00:00:23,690
speaker is here to speak about Harness Engineering, how to build software when humans steer and agents execute.

3
00:00:24,366 --> 00:00:31,110
Please join me in welcoming to the stage, member of technical staff at OpenAI, Ryan Lopopolo.

4
00:00:40,366 --> 00:00:41,100
Good morning, London.

5
00:00:46,446 --> 00:00:47,910
I'm super excited to be here today.

6
00:00:47,910 --> 00:00:49,130
I'm Ryan Lopopolo.

7
00:00:49,590 --> 00:00:55,550
And for the last nine months, I have had the privilege of building software exclusively with agents.

8
00:00:56,390 --> 00:00:57,910
I am a token billionaire.

9
00:00:58,350 --> 00:01:08,030
And I believe that in order for us to get into our AGI future, we want everybody to be token billionaires, to use the models to do the full job.

10
00:01:08,770 --> 00:01:17,130
And what that means is to lean into the idea that the models are capable of being a full software engineer.

11
00:01:17,130 --> 00:01:24,650
And I've lived that experience by banning my team from even touching their editors to have to work through the models in order to get the job done.

12
00:01:25,450 --> 00:01:37,490
And today, I'm going to talk to you a little bit about what it means to lean into that and operationalize the way you work, the code spaces you live in, and the processes on your teams in order to get the agents to do the full job.

13
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:45,252
I believe I'm preaching to the choir here when I say that the way we build software has changed.

14
00:01:45,838 --> 00:02:05,500
In the last six months, we have seen coding agents take over the world, and capability has continually advanced at a super-fast pace to have these models and the harnesses within which they live take more complex actions, do more complicated work with higher reliability over longer time horizons.

15
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:14,760
And the place we've gotten to here is that implementation is no longer the scarce resource of what it means to do the job of software engineering.

16
00:02:15,406 --> 00:02:16,120
Code is free.

17
00:02:16,600 --> 00:02:26,116
We have an abundance of code to solve the problems that we come across in our day-to-day as we run our teams, build software, and solve user problems.

18
00:02:27,900 --> 00:02:34,960
Hiring the hands on the keyboards as part of our teams is only constrained by GPU capacity and token budgets.

19
00:02:35,822 --> 00:02:46,200
And each engineer today in this room has access to 5, 50, or 5,000 engineers worth of capacity, 24-7 every day of the year.

20
00:02:47,182 --> 00:02:57,760
The only thing that needs to happen, our roles, is to figure out how to productively deploy these resources into our code and into our teams to make use of this new capacity.

21
00:02:59,150 --> 00:03:11,420
And in this world, skill sets are shifting more towards systems thinking, system design, and delegation in order to make use of this abundant capacity to produce code to solve problems.

22
00:03:11,420 --> 00:03:16,520
And there are three reasons that this happened, all of which happened in late 2025.

23
00:03:19,310 --> 00:03:25,860
For me, the magic moment was GPT 5.2, which, when it came out, was able to do the full job of a software engineer.

24
00:03:26,766 --> 00:03:37,560
The models at this point are good enough where they're isomorphic to you and I in terms of the ability to produce code at high quality that solve real user problems in real code bases.

25
00:03:39,920 --> 00:03:40,580
Code is free.

26
00:03:41,422 --> 00:03:53,660
And I know this is maybe a scary thing to hear, because code carries maintenance burden, but it's free to produce, free to refactor, and it is not a thing to get hung up on any more.

27
00:03:54,862 --> 00:04:03,640
We think of code as burden because it's a synchronous attention drain on the human engineers on our team, but the models are incredibly patient.

28
00:04:04,080 --> 00:04:05,420
they're infinitely parallel.

29
00:04:05,870 --> 00:04:15,332
so the ability to produce, maintain, refactor and delete code is no longer a forcing function on figuring out how to allocate resources on your engineering teams.

30
00:04:16,462 --> 00:04:29,320
So sort of be AGI-pilled here is to believe that the models are capable of producing every line of code we could ever possibly need, figuring out when to delete them, figuring out when to refactor them or make them more reliable.

31
00:04:29,998 --> 00:04:43,060
And it's your role as software engineers to figure out how to unblock your team of agents and humans driving those agents from being able to drive them over long horizon work to do the full job.

32
00:04:44,814 --> 00:04:47,700
The idea here is that every one of you is a staff engineer.

33
00:04:48,280 --> 00:04:54,060
You have as many team members as you can possibly drive concurrently and have tokens to support.

34
00:04:54,940 --> 00:05:07,020
And you need to look one day, one week, six months into the future to figure out what structures you need to put in place to productively harness this infinite capacity to produce code.

35
00:05:10,574 --> 00:05:21,040
The scarce resources in this world that we see today are three things, human time, human and model attention, and model context window.

36
00:05:22,060 --> 00:05:37,980
And in the world where human time and attention is scarce, the role is to think about where that time is going, figure out ways to productively automate it, and move that synchronous human time into higher leverage activities.

37
00:05:40,366 --> 00:05:47,480
In a world where human time is scarce, and human time is required to produce code, we have a stack rank.

38
00:05:48,180 --> 00:05:50,620
Things are either P0s or P2s.

39
00:05:50,940 --> 00:05:52,780
Those P3s will never get done.

40
00:05:53,360 --> 00:06:02,020
However, in a world where code is free and infinitely abundant, all those P3s get kicked off immediately, maybe 4x in parallel.

41
00:06:02,542 --> 00:06:05,680
We pick one that solves the problem, and in it goes.

42
00:06:07,054 --> 00:06:22,440
I've had the privilege of building a ton of agents internally at OpenAI to improve the productivity of my co-workers, and, when code is free, all these internal tools can have good localisation and internationalisation from day one.

43
00:06:23,214 --> 00:06:39,400
I can make tools that my colleagues in London, Dublin, Paris, Brussels, Zurich, and Munich are able to experience in their native languages without really having to trade against any of my other team's capacity in order to make high-quality tools.

44
00:06:40,814 --> 00:06:51,200
We should be working with the assumption that the best parts of software engineering that we all know, live, and breathe are available in any product that we could ever build all the time.

45
00:06:52,622 --> 00:06:55,860
Humans no longer need to concern themselves with implementation.

46
00:06:56,846 --> 00:07:00,760
The important thing is not the code, but the prompt and the guardrails that got you there.

47
00:07:01,486 --> 00:07:17,340
This is why leaving breadcrumbs, documentation, ADRs, persona-oriented documentation around what a good job looks like, all the historical logs of tickets and code reviews, this is the process that got you and your teams to the code and products that you have today.

48
00:07:17,780 --> 00:07:21,660
And this is what needs to happen in order to get your agents there as well.

49
00:07:23,054 --> 00:07:29,160
Your job is to build systems, software, and structures that enable your team to be successful.

50
00:07:29,900 --> 00:07:35,588
And to do that, we need to make them legible to those agents that are driving the implementation.

51
00:07:36,462 --> 00:07:50,580
That means structuring them in a way that's native to the agents, writing them in a way that is respecting of scarce context, which is this other scarce resource here, and figuring out ways to make the tokens that are required to do the job easy to predict.

52
00:07:51,118 --> 00:07:58,360
That means making things the same as much as possible so we can limit the amount of attention and the model needs to activate in order to do the job.

53
00:07:59,662 --> 00:08:02,500
Large-scale refactoring in this world is free.

54
00:08:02,926 --> 00:08:06,500
So making things the same is something that you are all able to do.

55
00:08:06,990 --> 00:08:13,360
There's never going to be a migration that hangs open for six months now that you can't get the last parts of the code base to do.

56
00:08:13,806 --> 00:08:16,860
Because you can just fire off 15 agents to drive that work to completion.

57
00:08:17,550 --> 00:08:19,680
This is what it means to have a migration, right?

58
00:08:19,700 --> 00:08:20,680
We can finish them now.

59
00:08:21,614 --> 00:08:21,860
Come on.

60
00:08:22,734 --> 00:08:23,120
That's good.

61
00:08:23,220 --> 00:08:23,400
Clap.

62
00:08:23,400 --> 00:08:33,640
But there's sort of this meta epistemological question here about what it means to do a good job.

63
00:08:34,320 --> 00:08:37,660
And doing a good job as a software engineer is hard.

64
00:08:38,606 --> 00:08:51,492
It requires us years of being in the industry to fully internalize what it means to write high-quality, maintainable, reliable code that our teammates are able to build on top of that is going to accrue leverage to the code base.

65
00:08:51,500 --> 00:09:04,300
To do a single patch well probably requires 500 little decisions along the way around the underspecified non-functional requirements that go into producing good code.

66
00:09:05,486 --> 00:09:15,780
The agents, the models during their training, have seen trillions of lines of code that make every possible choice of those non-functional requirements that you could ever imagine.

67
00:09:16,526 --> 00:09:27,900
So it's our job to specify those non-functional requirements, to write them down in a way that the agents can see, this is what it is to do a good, acceptable job that's going to produce a merge patch.

68
00:09:28,718 --> 00:09:38,060
And if the agents aren't doing that, it's our job to figure out ways to refine and restrict their output such that the code they write is acceptable.

69
00:09:38,798 --> 00:09:40,860
You can just simply say, do not produce slop.

70
00:09:41,582 --> 00:09:42,380
Don't accept slop.

71
00:09:42,400 --> 00:09:43,780
You won't get slop in your code base.

72
00:09:44,110 --> 00:09:51,900
But to do that requires taking short-term velocity hits in order to back up or double-click into a task to figure out what it is.

73
00:09:51,900 --> 00:10:04,960
the agents are struggling with in your environment, put the guardrails in place, so they stop making those mistakes, and then figure out ways to step back and spend your time on higher leverage activities.

74
00:10:04,960 --> 00:10:07,840
once you solve some of the blockers in the short term.

75
00:10:09,742 --> 00:10:15,220
When I think about empowering my team in this way, everyone is an expert in what it is they bring.

76
00:10:15,758 --> 00:10:33,560
I have a diverse, full-stack team that is experts in front-end architecture, back-end scalability, being product-minded, and each one of those different personas fleshes out the skill set of my team by bringing a different understanding, a different set of solves for those non-functional requirements.

77
00:10:34,542 --> 00:10:42,140
Getting teammates to write those down actually means that every engineer driving agents gets the best of every single person on my team.

78
00:10:42,702 --> 00:10:49,720
I don't need to block on low-signal code review in order to learn what it means to write a good QA plan.

79
00:10:50,894 --> 00:10:58,260
To have one engineer on my team document that in a durable way means every agent trajectory is going to get a good QA plan.

80
00:10:58,580 --> 00:11:02,780
And we can do this once in a high leverage way that we're able to stack on top of.

81
00:11:05,680 --> 00:11:08,240
So how can we get the agents to do a good job?

82
00:11:08,320 --> 00:11:20,360
What are some of the tools and techniques we have in order to essentially prompt inject our agents and continually remind them of what it means to make those specific choices that we expect around those non-functional requirements.

83
00:11:21,294 --> 00:11:22,720
And there's a bunch of ways we can do this.

84
00:11:23,406 --> 00:11:25,800
We can write good-agents.md files.

85
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:34,620
However, with auto-compaction, which is a thing that has continued to improve, GPT 5.4 and Codex is fantastic at auto-compaction.

86
00:11:34,700 --> 00:11:37,520
I essentially never have to write slash new anymore.

87
00:11:38,286 --> 00:11:46,080
I've got some pictures on my Twitter of me strapping my laptop into the back of my car so I can continue running inference while I'm commuting to and from work.

88
00:11:46,798 --> 00:11:55,060
and in this world, you have to kind of build for that expectation that context will get paged out over time.

89
00:11:55,502 --> 00:12:09,220
We need to be continually refreshing context as the agent goes about doing a task, and the ways we can do that are by having reviewer agents look at the code along the way through the lens of what it means to be successful, right?

90
00:12:09,838 --> 00:12:25,580
We have security and reliability review agents in our codebase that are continually running as part of every push in CI that look at those documentations and the proposed patch and do simple things like say, are there timeouts and retries on this bit of network code?

91
00:12:26,030 --> 00:12:30,720
Has the code that has been introduced have a secure interface that is impossible to misuse?

92
00:12:32,334 --> 00:12:50,080
I'm sure everyone here has been paged at some point for network code that failed in production, causing an outage that could have been remediated by a retry and a timeout, and I know I'm guilty of putting that retry and timeout in, merging the bug fix, and otherwise ignoring that.

93
00:12:50,638 --> 00:12:55,500
I am not a reliable reviewer or author of code with respect to this non-functional requirement.

94
00:12:56,120 --> 00:13:10,720
However, taking the time to write some docs, write a lint that is bespoke to my code base that is gonna look at every time I call fetch to make sure that there's a retry and a timeout wrapped around it, means I've durably solved this problem.

95
00:13:10,720 --> 00:13:22,080
And I'm able to do it because I lean on this axiom that code is free, that the agents are able to do a good job, that I can completely migrate the code base to solve this problem durably once and for all.

96
00:13:23,060 --> 00:13:36,080
And in order to kind of operate in this way, we need to step back and look at the durable classes of failures that the agents and the humans in the code base are making time after time.

97
00:13:37,134 --> 00:13:39,400
figure out why we're spending time on.

98
00:13:39,400 --> 00:13:50,240
it, devise a solution to systematically eliminate this class of misbehavior, and then continue to observe, refine, and make additional choices on those non-functional requirements.

99
00:13:51,406 --> 00:13:59,428
One really neat trick I use here is that you can write tests about the source code as well that are separate from lints.

100
00:13:59,900 --> 00:14:07,940
If we know that context is limited, we can write a test that limits the fact that files are no longer than 350 lines.

101
00:14:08,814 --> 00:14:19,420
We're adapting our code base to the harness, this to the models to do a little bit of engineering to be context efficient and squeeze more juice out of the model capability that we have today.

102
00:14:22,222 --> 00:14:32,890
The other things we can think about are providing good error messages that give actual remediation steps to the model and to humans for how to proceed next.

103
00:14:33,454 --> 00:14:45,230
It's not enough to say we've got a lint failure because we're awaiting in a loop or that we have an unknown at this deep part of the code base, and why is the model writing a function called isRecord?

104
00:14:45,838 --> 00:15:04,410
What we need to do is provide a prompt via a lint or a test failure that says, no, no, no, you shouldn't have an unknown here at all, because we parse don't validate at the edge, and you certainly have a type here which was derived from Zot, load-bearing infrastructure for our AI future.

105
00:15:10,094 --> 00:15:11,120
You can just prompt things.

106
00:15:11,940 --> 00:15:14,560
Everything I've talked about here today is a prompt.

