Because the way we have seen the agents work is to sometimes optimize for local coherence of a package rather than using our shared utilities and things like that.

178
00:22:24,800 --> 00:22:37,400
So having observed that behavior, we kind of have built a bunch of little pseudo-linter source code verification things that shake out some of that bad behavior so the humans don't get distracted paying attention to that in reviews.

179
00:22:37,870 --> 00:22:38,400
Stuff like that.

180
00:22:39,150 --> 00:22:47,840
But the setup optimizes for the agent to do the job and for the humans to not have to keep track of the high churn in the codebase.

181
00:22:49,102 --> 00:22:52,280
We kind of centralize our leverage around 5 to 10 skills.

182
00:22:52,960 --> 00:22:57,660
We don't go super wide on skills, preferring to make the existing skills better.

183
00:22:58,222 --> 00:23:06,420
Because at least I find that the infrastructure within the repository, all the local developer tools, change super frequently.

184
00:23:06,926 --> 00:23:10,000
And I don't really have the bandwidth to keep track of this.

185
00:23:10,094 --> 00:23:17,480
So we hide all that complexity beneath the skills that the human has to invoke and let the agent just kind of figure it out.

186
00:23:17,920 --> 00:23:28,440
One kind of neat thing here is when we move from using Chrome DevTools Protocol directly to having this like daemon thing, like, I didn't know that had happened for like three weeks.

187
00:23:28,960 --> 00:23:35,040
It was like totally fine, because Codex was able to do the thing, you know, with the documentation and things that we had in place.

188
00:23:35,790 --> 00:23:38,080
And part of this, you can get more detail in your article.

189
00:23:38,200 --> 00:23:41,260
So some background, you wrote a great piece called Harness Engineering.

190
00:23:41,710 --> 00:23:48,100
There's a whole section in there on how you thought about skills, thousands of skills versus simplifying it to just quite a few.

191
00:23:48,366 --> 00:23:54,660
But OK, continuing on, how do you stop yourself from over-engineering harnesses?

192
00:23:55,020 --> 00:24:01,280
And a little bit of a similar follow-up is, do you often build small tools for yourself, if ever?

193
00:24:01,902 --> 00:24:03,236
Do you build custom tools?

194
00:24:04,280 --> 00:24:15,080
Yeah, so I think this is kind of gesturing in the direction of the bitter lesson here, which is, how do I make sure the work that I do isn't completely obsoleted by an increase in model capability?

195
00:24:15,630 --> 00:24:28,240
And the way I have thought about that is doing sort of the bare minimum amount of context management to kind of pull in requirements for the agent to do an acceptable job over the course of its work.

196
00:24:28,900 --> 00:24:32,860
And context is a thing that I don't think will ever be obsoleted, right?

197
00:24:32,940 --> 00:24:39,680
Like the models must be told, like, the requirements of the task, which guardrails to pay attention to, these sorts of things.

198
00:24:40,302 --> 00:24:50,820
So a good harness is really operationalize around giving the model text at the right time so it can look at the work it has done and the information around what a good job looks like.

199
00:24:51,720 --> 00:24:55,820
And, you know, fundamentally the models are trained to follow instructions.

200
00:24:56,280 --> 00:25:00,120
All the harness should do is surface instructions to the model at the right time.

201
00:25:00,750 --> 00:25:03,640
So we do want to minimize that too, right?

202
00:25:03,660 --> 00:25:08,300
You don't want to front load all those instructions because then you kind of like overwhelm the agent.

203
00:25:08,942 --> 00:25:15,020
But all of these sort of requirements around what a good job do need to be paid attention to over the entire course of a PR, right?

204
00:25:15,342 --> 00:25:23,940
So figuring out ways to either defer or just in time surface those instructions is kind of what a good harness should do, right?

205
00:25:24,878 --> 00:25:37,120
If you know that you want your React components, right, to be decomposed so that they make good snapshot tests for individual more stateless pieces, right, you don't need to load that up front.

206
00:25:37,540 --> 00:25:41,900
Instead, you should kind of let the agent cook and prototype and experiment with the UI you want to build.

207
00:25:42,360 --> 00:25:45,920
And then at lint or test time, say, OK, you've done the work.

208
00:25:46,020 --> 00:25:56,920
In order to finish it, you have to break this apart so that your components are small and as stateless as possible and have local dependencies on hooks instead of prop drilling or whatever it is that you want the code to look like.

209
00:25:57,300 --> 00:26:00,260
And then the agent will say, oh, this is a new instruction for me.

210
00:26:00,420 --> 00:26:04,380
Let me take the patch as written, modify it to make sure that it adheres to the instructions.

211
00:26:04,910 --> 00:26:06,300
And then up it goes to GitHub.

212
00:26:06,862 --> 00:26:11,960
And this sort of thing is not going can be obsoleted by increases in model capability.

213
00:26:12,120 --> 00:26:16,540
It's really just about getting that right text, that right context to the agent at the right time.

214
00:26:17,966 --> 00:26:20,220
Can we talk about an example of a good harness?

215
00:26:20,782 --> 00:26:26,660
So a lot of people are asking about the Codex model, the Codex harness, how does that compare to other harnesses?

216
00:26:26,720 --> 00:26:31,260
So Cloud Code, Open Code, how do you guys take these decisions into play?

217
00:26:31,534 --> 00:26:39,620
You don't work directly on Codex, but if there's stuff you can speak about the Codex harness, is what you guys see as you architect it out.

218
00:26:40,320 --> 00:26:52,820
Yeah, so one thing that I think is super powerful is this notion that the labs are not just post-training the models, but post-training the models in the context of the harness in which they are primarily deployed in.

219
00:26:53,120 --> 00:27:10,240
Like, the apply patch tool, or the specific quoting semantics of how to invoke the bash tool are in the loop for the post-training process for the harnesses from the labs, which means there is leverage to be had by depending on these first-party harnesses directly.

220
00:27:10,640 --> 00:27:11,900
At least, this is what I believe.

221
00:27:13,000 --> 00:27:30,960
And as such, being able to direct through them via things like the SDK or manipulating the Codex app server directly means you get to ride the wave of all that leverage in post-training and instead focus on the parts that you care about, which is what correct code looks like.

222
00:27:33,006 --> 00:27:37,880
I kind of have high confidence that things like Cloud Code and Codex will continue to get better.

223
00:27:38,320 --> 00:27:42,040
That is the responsibility of, like, the teams working on these coding agents.

224
00:27:42,280 --> 00:27:54,560
So in my role, where I don't really want to focus on the coding harness at all, is figuring out ways to plug into them in ways that kind of, like, steer the agent.

225
00:27:55,150 --> 00:28:04,380
That means my job can sort of, like, move up to thinking about differences in model behavior between releases, rather than deeply understanding the nuts and bolts of the harness.

226
00:28:05,020 --> 00:28:14,020
Instead, I can think about what it means to, you know, drive the behavior that I want based on the observed behavior, rather than, like, the inner mechanics of the thing.

227
00:28:14,798 --> 00:28:20,340
It's a perfect follow-up to the next question, which is, do you have any recommendations for collaboration platforms?

228
00:28:20,620 --> 00:28:29,300
So when you're in the software development lifecycle, is there any platform that you use for agents, engineers, developers, all to collaborate on working on anything?

229
00:28:29,678 --> 00:28:30,940
Any tips, any tools?

230
00:28:32,720 --> 00:28:45,100
Yeah, so in this world, it has largely been just markdown files in the repository and GitHub that have been the primary sort of hub and spoke sort of thing.

231
00:28:46,030 --> 00:28:55,440
If you think about collaborating on a document, like you open Google Docs, you write something, you ask for feedback, people comment, you apply suggestions, these sorts of things.

232
00:28:55,726 --> 00:29:00,920
This is kind of like a little clean room environment just for this work artifact that you're producing.

233
00:29:01,320 --> 00:29:03,560
Like a PR kind of has a similar purpose.

234
00:29:03,880 --> 00:29:11,380
So we kind of treat that as a big hub and spoke broadcast domain where all of the agents and humans collaborate together.

235
00:29:12,420 --> 00:29:17,960
And because we optimize for throughput, we don't block on any sort of contribution to that.

236
00:29:18,260 --> 00:29:19,960
Like folks can either review or not.

237
00:29:20,060 --> 00:29:21,600
Agents can either review or not.

238
00:29:21,902 --> 00:29:37,140
the implementation agent can acknowledge, defer, or reject any feedback that it gets, really allowing each participant in the production of diffs to kind of make their own judgments around what it means to deliver, receive, respond to feedback.

239
00:29:37,760 --> 00:29:43,400
And this has the nice property of not putting the model in a box in a bunch of places.

240
00:29:43,480 --> 00:29:46,980
We want them to use their good reasoning sort of thing.

241
00:29:47,140 --> 00:30:04,860
So being super prescriptive around every bit of feedback must be addressed can kind of have this catastrophic failure mode of your coding agent being bullied by all of the reviewers, when really we want a bias toward code being accepted, not perfect, not drowning in minutiae, and these sorts of things.

242
00:30:06,318 --> 00:30:08,580
How should people get started with using coding agents?

243
00:30:08,900 --> 00:30:15,480
People that have been doing a lot of manually written code, how do they start to transition?

244
00:30:15,700 --> 00:30:16,680
What should they offload?

245
00:30:17,020 --> 00:30:21,540
How do they kind of come over that barrier of, OK, I'm still checking every PR.

246
00:30:21,806 --> 00:30:23,700
I'm copy-pasting from codecs.

247
00:30:24,174 --> 00:30:27,540
How should the average engineer start to use these tools?

248
00:30:28,718 --> 00:30:30,620
I think there's two ways to approach this problem.

249
00:30:30,900 --> 00:30:38,920
One is to start using the coding agents to improve your confidence in the code itself, as it is written today.

250
00:30:39,260 --> 00:30:50,760
I think we would all agree that more tests is probably a good thing, to assert that our programs are well-specified and behave correctly as our users interact with them is a good thing.

251
00:30:51,440 --> 00:31:00,300
And the agents are super good at looking at the existing code with some context around how it is meant to be used and writing tests that assert that behavior.

252
00:31:00,800 --> 00:31:15,300
So kind of using this to improve your confidence in the quality of the code will also increase the agent's ability to successfully navigate it, which means you don't have to worry as much around doing super detailed review of the agent output.

253
00:31:16,302 --> 00:31:20,860
The other way to think about this is to look at how you are spending your time.

254
00:31:21,806 --> 00:31:24,660
Is it, you know, staring at your editor, writing code?

255
00:31:24,900 --> 00:31:26,440
Is it waiting for tests to run?

256
00:31:26,780 --> 00:31:28,920
Is it waiting for human review feedback?

257
00:31:29,326 --> 00:31:30,280
Is CI slow?

258
00:31:30,980 --> 00:31:32,220
And you're, like, waiting on that.

259
00:31:32,300 --> 00:31:33,660
Maybe you have a ton of flaky tests.

260
00:31:33,980 --> 00:31:39,600
And using the agents to incrementally automate the parts where you are spending your time.

261
00:31:40,270 --> 00:31:51,556
Because ultimately, the high leverage parts of our jobs is to define the work that must be done, prioritize and schedule that work, and then effectively empower folks on our team to do that work.

262
00:31:52,380 --> 00:32:07,876
And the more and more we can delegate and move into sort of this sequencing and orchestration role, even if you just think about managing your teams, the more parallel and the more deeper individual executions of those delegations we're able to do.

263
00:32:08,910 --> 00:32:16,560
If I put primitives in place that make it super easy to like spin up ways to respond to events on my Kafka queue, right?

264
00:32:16,640 --> 00:32:22,080
Like I don't really need to be in the weeds with every engineer making sure they like implement a consumer correctly, right?

265
00:32:22,350 --> 00:32:28,840
And these same sort of like building block style techniques apply really well to the agents and stack really well, too.

266
00:32:29,966 --> 00:32:30,360
Fun one.

267
00:32:30,542 --> 00:32:31,960
How do you work with agents in your car?

268
00:32:35,120 --> 00:32:40,460
So I have not used the new voice mode that launched in CarPlay recently.

269
00:32:41,200 --> 00:32:55,660
I'm not ready for that, but usually what I'll do is kick off a task right before I leave the office, tether my laptop to my phone, buckle it into the back seat and let it cook in the 30 minutes it takes me to get home.

270
00:32:56,140 --> 00:33:13,700
Most of the time with the skills we invoke that tell the agent you're operating on a task, you go until the tests are green, I don't have to reach back there and poke yes, continue on to the thing, and I'm basically able to more fully saturate my day with token consumption.

271
00:33:15,310 --> 00:33:20,700
The dream here is that I actually have 50 agents running 24-7 and I don't have to interact with them at all.

272
00:33:21,580 --> 00:33:29,820
And the way to do that is to define the work well, figure out ways for it to automatically be scheduled, and remove myself from having to click the button, right?

273
00:33:30,126 --> 00:33:37,460
Every time I have to type continue to the agent is like a failure of the harness to provide enough context around what it means to continue to completion.

274
00:33:38,990 --> 00:33:39,220
Wow.

275
00:33:39,520 --> 00:33:41,080
Good, good statement at the end.

276
00:33:41,200 --> 00:33:43,840
Every time you have to interact with the agent is a failure.

277
00:33:44,460 --> 00:33:44,620
Okay.

278
00:33:44,942 --> 00:33:47,760
So the following question kind of scales this out, right?

279
00:33:47,840 --> 00:33:54,500
As your org knowledge map scales, what practical steps do you have to, like, enable progressive disclosure?

280
00:33:54,660 --> 00:34:00,420
So as you have a larger and larger code base, as you have more people, how do you scale your agents to work better with this?

281
00:34:00,910 --> 00:34:01,160
Yeah.

282
00:34:01,160 --> 00:34:16,200
So, when I sort of initially started this project that I was working on, blank repository, create Electron app, right, you know, v-single package, all this sort of stuff, and eventually ended up with a mess, right?

283
00:34:16,260 --> 00:34:23,100
Because there's no package privacy that allows me to enforce invariance around what APIs are public versus which ones are not.

284
00:34:23,534 --> 00:34:30,860
The agent didn't have, like, concrete hooks in the file system to determine which domains were separate from the other ones.

285
00:34:31,278 --> 00:34:52,760
So we ended up going like full 10,000 engineer organization heavy on the architecture, 750 packages in the PMPM, workspace, isolated by business, logic domain or layer of the stack, individual small util packages that encapsulate reusable functionality that we'd linked on being used, that we can encode leverage in.

286
00:34:53,358 --> 00:35:08,516
And I do think that like in this world, Even if you don't actually have microservices, structuring your repositories in ways that you can actually scope the directory subtree you are looking in to be able to do most of the change helps.

287
00:35:11,150 --> 00:35:17,720
And code in the file system is also text, which means it's effectively prompts that you're giving to your coding agent.

288
00:35:18,440 --> 00:35:30,212
So making the code as much the same as possible kind of makes it so that regardless of where or in the repository your agent is looking, it develops a ton of transferable context.

289
00:35:30,720 --> 00:35:35,588
You should have one way to do a bounded concurrency helper.

290
00:35:35,982 --> 00:35:42,000
You should have one way to construct observable and instrumented side-effectful command.

291
00:35:42,414 --> 00:35:43,860
You should have one ORM.

292
00:35:44,160 --> 00:35:45,720
You should have one programming language.

293
00:35:45,840 --> 00:35:47,600
You should have one way of writing CI scripts.

294
00:35:47,700 --> 00:35:50,500
You should have one way of adding additional lint rules, these sorts of things.

295
00:35:50,862 --> 00:35:58,420
Because it means that the tokens that you want the model to produce, are easier to predict, and more consistently predicted regardless of where it looks.

296
00:35:59,726 --> 00:36:07,800
So I would say, figure out ways to structure the code so it is local to a subtree in a repository for most of the ways you would interact with that system.

297
00:36:08,142 --> 00:36:12,640
And then figure out a way to use these agents to completely migrate the code base to be the same.

298
00:36:13,360 --> 00:36:19,080
Empower someone on your team to be a dictator, to say this is the way it must be done, or figure that out together.

299
00:36:20,820 --> 00:36:25,956
and write it down, evolve the code so that it reflects that reality, these sorts of things.

300
00:36:26,798 --> 00:36:28,680
We've got a few questions on code review.

301
00:36:28,920 --> 00:36:32,060
How do you approach code review now that you have such high velocity?

302
00:36:32,820 --> 00:36:34,120
Do you just not read the code?

303
00:36:34,240 --> 00:36:36,080
Do you just trust the test coverage?

304
00:36:36,720 --> 00:36:38,060
How do you write good tests?

305
00:36:38,340 --> 00:36:45,348
How do you offload that sigma of like, you have a mental blocker, I need to manually check everything before I merge PRs?

306
00:36:46,660 --> 00:37:01,980
So, that same sort of idea where you have to look at where you're spending your time and figure out ways to spend less of it, you know, when we started, right, the first thing to do was figure out how to get the agent reliably producing code that we would accept.

307
00:37:02,542 --> 00:37:12,840
And a big challenge we ran into is with each engineer producing three to five PRs per day, even on a team of three, merge conflicts were super miserable, right?

308
00:37:12,840 --> 00:37:15,160
because these PRs tended to be pretty big.

309
00:37:15,440 --> 00:37:18,380
We were working on the same parts of the code base.

310
00:37:18,542 --> 00:37:20,880
So that's where we moved in two directions.

311
00:37:21,100 --> 00:37:32,680
One was to like tree out the code a bit more to minimize these merge conflicts, but also minimize the amount of time PRs were open so that we were reducing the likelihood of a merge conflict actually occurring.

312
00:37:33,280 --> 00:37:42,660
And the reason PRs were staying open so long was because we needed a code review you, because humans were being the blocker in this scenario.

313
00:37:43,720 --> 00:37:48,640
So, in order to do that piece automatically.

314
00:37:49,166 --> 00:37:53,360
I essentially asked every engineer on the team to take one day a week, Fridays.

315
00:37:53,774 --> 00:38:08,380
we called it garbage collection day, where our entire job was to take every bit of slop we had observed over the course of the week that was making a PR difficult to merge and figure out ways to categorically eliminate it.

316
00:38:08,398 --> 00:38:26,500
From ever happening in the first place, which is where we kind of started closing this loop between the feedback that humans were giving on the PR indicates some context failure on behalf of the agent, getting that into the repository, and then figuring out ways to automatically prompt inject the agent so that it would self-heal when it produced this bad behavior.

317
00:38:26,920 --> 00:38:44,380
And this is kind of how you go from synchronous human time spent giving feedback as code review to comments, to documentation in the repository, to automatically surfing this documentation, either via a failing test or a viewer agent who is primed to review the code as written in the context of these docs.

318
00:38:45,518 --> 00:38:50,600
But all of that happens by putting those docs in a single place that all these processes are able to attach to.

319
00:38:53,806 --> 00:39:06,480
We kind of asked folks to basically bucket the types of review feedback they were giving into the persona they were operating as, like front-end architect, you know, reliability, engineer, scalability sort of thing.

320
00:39:06,862 --> 00:39:13,360
And then basically for each of those personas, we spun up a review agent that gets triggered on every push that says, is this code good?

321
00:39:13,518 --> 00:39:19,940
Surface any P2s or above that would block this PR from merging based on these documentation that says what good looks like.

322
00:39:21,100 --> 00:39:27,260
And with that, and just continuously appending to these files, we started to see slop reduce, reduce, reduce.

323
00:39:27,260 --> 00:39:30,800
People have questions about your billion tokens.

324
00:39:31,280 --> 00:39:33,060
Where do you think those are split up?

325
00:39:33,140 --> 00:39:35,100
So how much of it is on code review?

326
00:39:35,580 --> 00:39:38,400
Where is the majority of that usage coming from?

327
00:39:38,740 --> 00:39:45,640
And a follow-up, for people that are just getting started, say they've jumped and done a $200 pro plan, right?

328
00:39:45,934 --> 00:39:51,280
If you had to cut your usage by a fifth, how should people maximize their usage, right?

329
00:39:51,280 --> 00:39:52,560
You run into usage limits.

330
00:39:53,300 --> 00:40:01,500
You don't want to just copy-paste million lines of code every six hours, no prompt cache hit, but how should we think about that?

331
00:40:01,920 --> 00:40:02,120
Yeah.

332
00:40:02,542 --> 00:40:06,500
So I would say probably...

333
00:40:08,014 --> 00:40:18,640
It's probably a third, a third, a third between, like, planning, ticket curation, documentation, implementation, and stuff that runs in CI.

334
00:40:18,894 --> 00:40:19,620
Do you use plan mode?

335
00:40:21,820 --> 00:40:31,460
I've used exec plans, which was kind of like an early version of this that we published, which is sort of like a proto-skill that says this is how you should structure a plan with milestones and acceptance criteria.

336
00:40:33,038 --> 00:40:35,600
I haven't really used plan mode as part of the harness at all.

337
00:40:35,800 --> 00:40:45,440
My sort of expectation here is that I should be able to drop a ticket in and have it do the job anyway without diverting through a plan, because most of the time I'm never going to read it anyway.

338
00:40:46,180 --> 00:40:54,820
So I find that if you do use a plan and you approve it without reading it at all, you're actually encoding a bunch of instructions that you don't necessarily all you want followed.

339
00:40:55,440 --> 00:41:09,444
So if you are going to use plans, my recommendation is to push those up as single PRs with just the plan, where you actually have humans review every line of it and block on human approval before they get merged and then kicked off.

340
00:41:10,510 --> 00:41:17,180
Because you're effectively potentially wasting your time on a rollout with instructions that are bad.

341
00:41:17,646 --> 00:41:20,080
So you want to minimize the time that happens.

342
00:41:20,206 --> 00:41:30,920
But I do think that kind of getting tokens to be spent in CI is a necessary part here, because writing code no longer is the hard part.

343
00:41:31,100 --> 00:41:38,260
Like, getting code accepted and advancing the code and product forward is, like, what it takes to make that written code be valuable.

344
00:41:39,060 --> 00:41:43,960
And you know, kind of have all heard the aphorism that, like, you know, senior engineers give good code reviews.

345
00:41:44,040 --> 00:41:46,680
Like, we expect our senior engineers as agents to do the same.

346
00:41:47,182 --> 00:41:52,200
Someone asked, is code a disposable build artifact?

347
00:41:53,500 --> 00:41:53,740
Yes.

348
00:41:54,820 --> 00:42:00,780
I think we touch on this with Symfony, which is sort of this agent orchestrator that we released.

349
00:42:00,880 --> 00:42:10,820
This idea that, you know, we can publish a library that's actually a super well-defined spec that the code is a compiled artifact of.

350
00:42:11,182 --> 00:42:18,840
And I think like using LLM as fuzzy compiler is like an interesting mental model to have, right?

351
00:42:18,900 --> 00:42:31,160
Like all of the context that we're putting in the code base for harness engineering is effectively like constraints and optimization passes on which code is acceptable to build in the first place.

352
00:42:31,566 --> 00:42:40,260
And this is pretty similar to like the static analysis and optimization passes that something like LLVM would do in the process of compiling Rust code.

353
00:42:41,120 --> 00:42:52,340
And sort of swapping out one model for another is sort of like changing your code generation backend from, you know, LLVM to Cranelift in the Rust compiler.

354
00:42:52,782 --> 00:43:06,596
And you would expect that all of the sort of rules around what acceptable Rust code looks like produce valid sound machine code at the back, even if the generation process is different and you end up with different x86 instructions.

355
00:43:07,182 --> 00:43:11,380
So same sort of mindset for LLMs, of swapping out different models sort of thing.

356
00:43:11,694 --> 00:43:20,740
We want the structure around the code to basically limit how it is written to things that would be acceptable to us.

357
00:43:21,838 --> 00:43:26,200
At a high level, can you give us a picture of what future you're building for?

358
00:43:26,542 --> 00:43:27,700
Does context still matter?

359
00:43:28,110 --> 00:43:31,680
How do people do engineering, harness engineering, context engineering?

360
00:43:32,046 --> 00:43:33,120
What does the future look like?

361
00:43:35,790 --> 00:43:48,760
Sort of the future that I want to build toward here is where I'm able to take a token budget and a quarter, a half, or a year's worth of work.

362
00:43:50,126 --> 00:43:52,180
take the human input to rank.

363
00:43:52,238 --> 00:44:05,700
what is most important, success, metrics, reliability metrics, give it to the machines, and have them continually work and advance my product forward without my hands explicitly on the wheels at all.

364
00:44:05,742 --> 00:44:21,920
As we have gone through very early prototyping to internal alpha, internal beta, external alpha, I kind of have felt that new parts of the software engineering process have kind of started from zero.

365
00:44:22,100 --> 00:44:31,980
and we've had to build up capability, kind of like these pentagonal personality charts, where I spike in this direction, maybe I'm weak over here.

366
00:44:31,980 --> 00:44:44,080
And, you know, when we get to deployed software for the first time, right, the agent's ability to do, like, QA smoke testing on our built artifacts before they're promoted to distribution was weak.

367
00:44:44,180 --> 00:44:45,620
We hadn't invested any time in this.

368
00:44:45,680 --> 00:44:46,280
There were no docs.

369
00:44:46,380 --> 00:44:56,300
There were no tools that the agents could use to, like, download the built artifact, launch it, poke around to make sure that our, like, most critical user journeys were well validated and tested.

370
00:44:56,300 --> 00:45:04,740
So, because I don't want to be touching the computer, we needed to figure out, like, ways for the agents to build themselves tools to do that part.

371
00:45:06,860 --> 00:45:11,160
And there's a whole universe of software engineering outside of writing code, right?

372
00:45:11,220 --> 00:45:14,340
Like, I am triaging user feedback, I'm triaging pages.

373
00:45:14,958 --> 00:45:20,380
I am making sure that we don't have any PII leaking in the logs in production.

374
00:45:20,878 --> 00:45:23,460
I'm making sure that, like, the Twitter vibes are good.

375
00:45:23,460 --> 00:45:36,860
and people are enjoying my software, that our user operations staff are supported with well-written run books that allow them to triage and mitigate high-volume user issues, and then moving that into the code itself.

376
00:45:36,860 --> 00:45:38,140
so they don't happen in the first place.

377
00:45:38,680 --> 00:45:57,220
And as I no longer have to produce code, like, my mind can shift to these other higher-level or more squishy activities, but the agents are good enough to do these things, too, and figuring out how to write down the processes and the acceptance criteria becomes like the sort of like metaprogramming part of the job using these agents.

378
00:45:57,742 --> 00:45:58,920
That's a great way to end it.

379
00:45:58,980 --> 00:45:59,820
What an exciting feature.

380
00:46:00,206 --> 00:46:01,100
Give it up for Ryan, guys.

381
00:46:01,240 --> 00:46:01,820
Thank you, folks.

382
00:46:01,820 --> 00:46:01,860
Thank you.

